1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Mar–May in Montréal has warmed by about 0.25 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.8 °C in all. There is a modest but consistent El Niño signal on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Mar–May tends to run warmer. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 60% of a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 28 years of the long-term trend. Mar–May rainfall shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a modest but consistent effect toward a drier season with less intense heavy-rain days, and this year's shift is a noticeable tilt. Relative humidity has fallen by about 0.7 points per decade, roughly 5 points since 1951; because warmer air holds more moisture, this does not necessarily mean the air is drier. For Mar–May 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is likely (about 80% chance), about 1.4 °C above the 1991–2020 average; a less humid season than normal is likely (about 70% chance), mostly because of the long-term trend rather than El Niño. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":6.199,"trend_per_decade":0